# GIT HOOKS MUST STAY WITH UNIX LINE ENDINGS, ALWAYS.
#
# On Windows `core.autocrlf` is `true` by default (verified on this machine):
# git converts text files to CRLF when it checks them out. A `pre-push` with
# CRLF is passed to `sh`, which reads the first line as «/bin/sh\r» and answers
#
#     bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M: no such file or directory
#
# The hook does not start, the push goes ahead all the same, and **there is no message that
# says the check was skipped**: the push succeeds, which is exactly what
# would have happened without a hook. It is the defect that gives no error, applied
# to a defense — the defense disappears and the symptom is «all is well».
#
# It is not hypothetical: it happens at the first clone on any Windows machine,
# that is, on all those on which this project runs.
Checks/hooks/* text eol=lf

# Shell scripts in general, for the same reason.
*.sh text eol=lf

# NOTHING is declared for the `.ps1` files: with `core.autocrlf=true` they already arrive
# in CRLF, which is what is needed, and adding a line here would mean
# renormalizing files that are fine — a huge diff in exchange for nothing.
# A rule that repeats the default behavior is only a way of
# breaking something the day the default changes.

# SOLIDWORKS files are binary and must not be touched by any conversion:
# a `.sldprt` with its line endings «repaired» is a broken file.
*.sldprt binary
*.slddrw binary
*.sldasm binary
*.sldlfp binary
*.sldmat binary

# THE CATALOG STAYS WITH UNIX LINE ENDINGS, as `Oce.Solid.Agent.Catalog.exe` writes it.
# Measured on 25/9/2026 on a clean clone from GitHub: git checked it out in
# CRLF (153,225 bytes), the generator rewrote it in LF (150,270), and `build.ps1`
# told whoever cloned «catalog.json REGENERATED: it was behind the
# C#» — a false alarm, same content line by line. A warning that always trips
# is learned not to be read, and the day it is true it passes.
Tools/catalog.json text eol=lf